  <title>Hello, Research Blog</title>
  <link>https://hessamla.github.io/blog/posts/2026-09-07-welcome/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 




<p>This is the first post on the research blog, and mostly a test of the toolchain: math via MathJax, citations via BibTeX, and an executable code cell producing a figure — the things this blog needs for writing up graph ML experiments.</p>
<section id="math" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="math">Math</h2>
<p>A simple way to think about graph embedding is as a map <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?f:%20V%20%5Cto%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D%5Ed"> from nodes to vectors that preserves structural similarity. One common training objective, following the skip-gram formulation used in <span class="citation" data-cites="grover2016node2vec">Grover and Leskovec (2016)</span>, is</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cmathcal%7BL%7D%20=%20-%5Csum_%7Bu%20%5Cin%20V%7D%20%5Csum_%7Bv%20%5Cin%20N(u)%7D%20%5Clog%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cexp(f(u)%5E%5Ctop%20f(v))%7D%7B%5Csum_%7Bw%20%5Cin%20V%7D%20%5Cexp(f(u)%5E%5Ctop%20f(w))%7D.%0A"></p>
<p>Graph convolutional approaches such as <span class="citation" data-cites="kipf2017semi">Kipf and Welling (2017)</span> instead learn embeddings by propagating and transforming neighbor features layer by layer:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AH%5E%7B(l+1)%7D%20=%20%5Csigma%5Cleft(%5Ctilde%7BD%7D%5E%7B-%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B2%7D%7D%20%5Ctilde%7BA%7D%20%5Ctilde%7BD%7D%5E%7B-%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B2%7D%7D%20H%5E%7B(l)%7D%20W%5E%7B(l)%7D%5Cright).%0A"></p>
</section>
