<item>
  <title>Neighbor Explosion, Measured</title>
  <link>https://hessamla.github.io/blog/posts/2026-09-07-neighbor-explosion/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 




<p>The awkward thing about mini-batch training for GNNs is that a mini-batch isn’t self-contained. To compute one node’s representation at layer <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L">, you need its neighbors’ representations at layer <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L-1">, which need <em>their</em> neighbors at <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L-2">, and so on. The batch quietly drags in an <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L">-hop neighborhood.</p>
<section id="the-growth-term" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-growth-term">The growth term</h2>
<p>For a graph with average degree <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?d">, the expected size of a node’s <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L">-hop neighborhood, ignoring overlap, is</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%7C%5Cmathcal%7BN%7D_L(v)%7C%20%5C;%5Capprox%5C;%20%5Csum_%7Bl=1%7D%5E%7BL%7D%20d%5E%7B%5C,l%7D%20%5C;=%5C;%20%5Cfrac%7Bd%5E%7B%5C,L+1%7D%20-%20d%7D%7Bd%20-%201%7D,%0A"></p>
<p>which is exponential in depth. Overlap makes the real number smaller — but on graphs with small diameter, the thing that eventually bounds it is <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?N"> itself, the whole graph. That’s the failure mode: at some depth, “the neighborhood” and “the dataset” stop being different things.</p>
<p>Neighbor sampling <span class="citation" data-cites="hamilton2017inductive">(Hamilton et al. 2017)</span> caps the branching instead of the result. Sampling at most <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?r_l"> neighbors at layer <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?l"> bounds the receptive field by <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cprod_%7Bl=1%7D%5E%7BL%7D%20r_l">, independent of the graph’s actual degree distribution. It’s a bound you choose rather than one the data hands you.</p>
</section>
<section id="measuring-it" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="measuring-it">Measuring it</h2>
<p>Rather than trust the closed form, here’s the real count on a scale-free synthetic graph — 20,000 nodes, preferential attachment, so the degree distribution is heavy-tailed the way real networks tend to be. BFS from a sample of seed nodes, counting distinct nodes reached within <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L"> hops:</p>
